WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.610
- Unions seem to be
having a bit of a moment.

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.010
You may have heard about
the successes of organizers

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:09.000
at Amazon warehouses or
Starbucks coffee shops, places

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.690
that have historically been
very difficult to unionize.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.150
President Joe Biden has
promised to be the most

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.660
pro-union president in
American history, and as

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.990
of this filming, the entertainment
industry is still hobbled

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.090
by a protracted strike by writers

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:27.240
and actors flexing their unionized muscle.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.150
- In addition to this press,
or perhaps because of it

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.150
public attitudes towards
organized labor are higher

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.310
than they've been in almost 60 years.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.730
71% of Americans now
approve of labor unions

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.150
up from a low of 48% in 2009.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:42.983
- Wow.

00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:44.880
So people really like unions

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.650
and they really want to be in them.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:47.790
Are we seeing a return

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.640
to the union glory days of
the forties and fifties?

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:51.540
- Not really.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.160
In fact, not at all.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.500
Actual union membership as a percentage

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.980
of the workforce is at its
lowest level of all time.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.240
Despite the good PR,

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.230
America has never been less
unionized than it is right now.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.370
- Why is that?

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.980
Well, unlike many other
countries where unions

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.040
are historically accepted and normalized,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.310
America has a long tradition
of putting organized labor

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.700
at the center of our political
battlefield, resulting

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.720
in a lot of complex and
sometimes confusing legislation.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.480
- And beyond that, the
economic, technological

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:25.890
and even cultural changes

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.710
of the last several decades
have created a landscape

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.340
less hospitable to collective
bargaining than ever before.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.920
Despite their current
popularity, the survival

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.593
of unions has yet to be negotiated.

00:01:38.616 --> 00:01:41.033
(soft music)

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.290
- Unions date all the way
back to the 18th century.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.110
When the Industrial Revolution
crammed large numbers

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.820
of workers into factories,

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.830
inadvertently giving
them the opportunity to

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.350
share their frustrations and interests.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.350
They used two main tactics
to increase their pay

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.210
and improve working conditions:

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:06.330
Collective bargaining, by
which all members benefited

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.250
from the union's negotiations,

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.820
and strikes, which used work stoppages to

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:15.180
demonstrate the employer's
reliance on their workforce.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.510
- Organized Labor's heyday
was in the 1940s and fifties.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.640
Thanks, in part to governmental policies,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.180
like the National Labor
Relations Act of 1935,

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.170
which made it easier

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.200
and safer to organize unions
without being spied on,

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.200
harassed or otherwise
punished by employers.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.490
Before that, it was common

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:35.640
for employers to use threats,
intimidation, even violence

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.800
to keep their employees from organizing.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.520
By the 1950s, about one in
three American workers belonged

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.010
to a union, which is
credited as a key factor

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:47.220
in that decade's relatively
low economic inequality.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.200
- But even back then, some business

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.770
and political leaders were
working to weaken union's power.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.800
The Taft Hartley Act passed in 1947

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.020
limited union's bargaining strategies,

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.820
like restricting certain kinds

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.790
of boycotts and made it
easier for employers to

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:07.770
use non-union labor known as
scabs to circumvent strikes.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:11.130
Union leaders were also
required to sign affidavits

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.950
denouncing any communist sympathies.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.720
And anti-union propaganda

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.090
exploited white Southerners fear

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.910
of desegregation to stymie the spread

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.910
of unions in the textile
industries of the south.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:24.750
- The stagflation

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:28.380
of the 1970s severely damaged
organized labor's power

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:29.820
and public image.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.040
Business leaders managed
to convince politicians

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.420
and even their employees

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.050
that unions were standing in
the way of economic recovery.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.750
Then in 1981, when Ronald Reagan,

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:43.860
who had once been a union
leader himself, summarily fired

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.070
over 11,000 striking air
traffic control workers,

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:48.690
it was seen as a potentially

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:51.120
fatal blow to the labor movement.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.980
If the American president
could take such an

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.460
unequivocal stance against
the rights of striking workers

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.310
without any negative feedback
from the voting public,

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:02.370
perhaps union's best days
were really behind them.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:06.120
- It's difficult to quantify
union's effect on the economy

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.760
but there does seem to be
a remarkable correlation

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:12.090
between union membership
and low levels of income

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:15.750
and equality, and the decades
of peak union strength

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.540
were not exactly bad for business either.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.520
Perhaps that's why in recent years

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:23.880
as inequality has reached
historic levels and a small number

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.970
of corporations dominate
so much of our daily lives,

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:28.080
there's been a renewed interest

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.780
in unions as a potential counterbalance.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:33.960
- But the American economy and
the world are very different

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.730
than they were 60 years ago.

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.160
For one thing, globalization has taken one

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.850
of organized labor's primary
tactics off the table.

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:43.890
If a factory owner doesn't want to give

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:45.720
into his striking workers' demands,

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.300
he can just move the factory overseas

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.790
to a country where
unions aren't a problem.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:54.330
America has lost a ton of
manufacturing jobs as a result

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:57.000
and the ones that are
left are less secure.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.060
- American labor law is
also much less hospitable

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.970
to unions than in other
industrialized nations.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:04.290
Right to work laws

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.740
passed in 28 states allow employees to opt

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:11.190
out of paying union dues
even if they benefit

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:14.310
from the higher pay the
union negotiated for them

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:17.610
which has crippled many
union's ability to operate.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:19.170
- And unlike in other countries

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:20.940
where organizers can negotiate

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.700
on behalf of all the
workers in an entire company

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.610
or even an entire industry, labor leaders

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:30.270
in America must organize
each individual workplace.

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:31.800
Starbucks by Starbucks,

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.230
Amazon warehouse by Amazon Warehouse.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:37.830
Each one of these campaigns is
expensive and time consuming

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:41.010
for already underpaid
employees, and they face armies

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:43.520
of high price lawyers
and consultants hired

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.770
by the company to stop them at all costs.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.050
- There's another cultural trend working

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:52.590
against labor organizing: loneliness.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:54.570
In his book Bowling Alone,

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:57.570
the political scientist
Robert Putnam, chronicled how

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:01.680
Americans are becoming more
isolated, more suspicious

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:04.530
and less likely to
participate in social groups

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:08.700
like churches, bowling
leagues, and yes, unions.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.760
- Forming a union is a
daunting and scary challenge

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:15.060
which requires strong bonds
of trust and solidarity,

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:18.510
what social scientists
refer to as social capital.

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:19.950
This was easier when hundreds

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:22.890
of people worked side by
side in big factories.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:26.310
But today, even if our
employer is a giant corporation

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:27.420
we're more likely to work

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:31.410
in a small atomized
environment or even from home.

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.000
Turnover is much more frequent.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.340
How can you convince
someone to help unionize

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:37.920
a place they don't expect
to be at very long?

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.800
And we're more likely to
spend our lunch breaks staring

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.770
at screens rather than
chatting with coworkers.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.260
- And yet, despite all these obstacles

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:49.680
the will to unionize is alive and kicking.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.290
According to the National
Labor Relations Board

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:55.740
there was a 53% increase
of union petitions filed

00:06:55.740 --> 00:07:00.740
in 2022 and strikes were up
52% from the previous year.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.390
Activists are fighting to pass the

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:05.730
Protecting the Right to Organize Act

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:06.780
which would restrict some

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:10.800
of the tactics employers use
to thwart unionizing efforts.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:12.990
But with such a divided Congress

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:15.600
its chances are hazy at best.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.910
- As the economist Suresh Naidu writes,

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:21.540
between the employer side
advantages given by US labor law

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:23.850
and diminished workplace social capital

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:27.270
it is difficult to see a
path to a persistent increase

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:29.880
in union density that is not concomitant

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:31.980
with a rewiring of workplace networks

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:34.710
and a transformation
of American labor law.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:37.320
- Seems tough, but the silver lining is

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:40.080
that any such transformation would start

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.270
with a rise in public support.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:45.000
- Which is exactly what we're seeing now.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.600
- [Both] And that's our 2 cents.

